1
00:00:04,187 --> 00:00:12,607
Answer a question with a report — Insights ▸ Reports — Start from the question, find the report that answers it, and read it properly.

2
00:00:20,119 --> 00:00:30,368
Insights ▸ Reports groups reports by the question they answer: operational, financial, insurance, patient, provider, compliance.

3
00:00:30,368 --> 00:00:40,840
So start from the question. "How many people are not turning up?" is an operational question.

4
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:48,594
The No-Show Report opens on the numbers that answer it: how many were scheduled, how many did not arrive, and the rate.

5
00:00:48,594 --> 00:00:59,549
Late cancellations sit alongside them, because a slot cancelled an hour before is lost just as completely.

6
00:00:59,549 --> 00:01:06,650
Set the date range before you read the rate. A percentage with no period attached means nothing.

7
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:13,508
Ninety days smooths out a bad week. Seven days is what you look at when you have just changed something.

8
00:01:13,508 --> 00:01:21,650
The chart is the trend: no-shows per day. One bad Monday is noise — the same day every week is a pattern.

9
00:01:21,650 --> 00:01:29,988
Switch between bar, line and area if a different shape makes the pattern easier to see.

10
00:01:29,988 --> 00:01:37,792
Detailed Records is the part that lets you act: who missed, which provider, what it was for, and their phone number.

11
00:01:37,792 --> 00:01:50,384
A rate tells you the size of the problem. The names tell you what to do about it — and one patient appearing three times is its own answer.

12
00:01:50,384 --> 00:01:58,658
Export to PDF for a meeting or Excel when someone needs to work the list. Both carry the range you set.

13
00:02:00,397 --> 00:02:17,427
